1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,894
NEIOS is developer infrastructure for N-ATLAS — an SDK, a governance toolkit, an evaluation harness and a local speech service that take you from a raw model to a system you can actually deploy.

2
00:00:20,894 --> 00:00:26,064
This is the reference app proving it end to end.

3
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:51,455
For a developer, N-ATLAS arrives as an endpoint — but a real system needs intake structure, a policy check before the model ever runs, output validation, field-level provenance, human approval and an audit trail.

4
00:00:51,455 --> 00:01:01,172
NEIOS ships all of that as packages, plus a playground to test against the live model.

5
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:07,798
Voice input is part of the infrastructure too.

6
00:01:07,798 --> 00:01:34,516
This service runs the four official N-ATLAS speech checkpoints locally and exposes the official transcriptions API — so any N-ATLAS app can accept spoken Hausa, Yoruba, Igbo or Nigerian English without standing up its own ASR stack.

7
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:56,994
One real call through the pipeline: deterministic policy fires first, then the model drafts, then validators repair what they can prove — watch the corrected fields — and every value keeps a provenance tag.

8
00:01:56,994 --> 00:02:03,312
Nothing is mocked; the audit log gets a real model request ID.

9
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:13,900
The same pipeline in Hausa, Yoruba and Igbo — four live model calls, same controls.

10
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:22,890
When extraction slips, validation catches it and the draft still waits on a human.

11
00:02:22,890 --> 00:02:29,688
Multilingual support is inherited from N-ATLAS, not bolted on.

12
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:51,973
And because infrastructure has to be measurable: a golden-fixture harness scores the pipeline against real N-ATLAS — ten cases covering four languages, an injection attempt, missing fields and an arithmetic trap.

13
00:02:51,973 --> 00:02:59,059
Ten for ten, mean ninety-three, every run pinned to a prompt version.

14
00:02:59,059 --> 00:03:01,832
Any developer can rerun it.

15
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:18,373
The safety case: a wire-transfer request is blocked by policy before a single token is generated — the model never sees it, and the refusal is still audited.

16
00:03:18,373 --> 00:03:24,752
That's the difference between prompting a model and governing one.

17
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:30,549
Consequential output waits for a person.

18
00:03:30,549 --> 00:03:48,160
Approval is one-shot and binding, and the whole trail — request, model response, repairs, decision — lands in one append-only log your auditors can actually read.

19
00:03:49,700 --> 00:04:06,569
NEIOS: the SDK, governance toolkit, evaluation harness and ASR shim for N-ATLAS — plus a working reference app in four languages.

20
00:04:06,569 --> 00:04:15,069
Clone it, point it at your endpoint, and ship something governed.

21
00:04:15,069 --> 00:04:17,684
Built for NAIC 2026.